  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe address the problem by optimizing sequences over a simplex (unit-sum nonnegative vectors) to minimize the AC1 upper bound. The objective is the max autocorrelation component, which is convex and scalable to high dimensions. Our method combines projected subgradient descent with adaptive learning rates and automatic restarts. Compared to the parent program's steepest descent, this leverages faster convergence on convex problems and systematically escapes local minima.\n\nThe quadratic time complexity of autocorrelation computation is managed by:\n1. FFT for improvement estimation\n2. Moving averages for gradient smoothing\n3. Phase-based budget allocation\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Projected simplex projection function (using sorting and cumulative sums)\n\n2. Objective computation:\n   - Convolve using FFT\n   - Track active set via max value (numerical tolerance)\n   - Gradient is average of active constraint gradients\n\n3. Optimization loop:\n   - Track best candidate and score since last restart\n   - Phase allocation: coarse exploration, fine tuning, restart phases\n   - Momentum helper even on the simplex\n\n4. Restart:\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Continuous time monitoring (scandir,wfr SSU  all routes)\n   - falls back to uniform distribution\n   - handles numerical issues automatically\n   - granular budget control per phase\n\n6. Return contract:\n   - Simpl corewiMS T_sum at least 0.01\n   - Coefficient bounds (max of 100 without scaling)\n   - Fallbeck is uniform distribution\n   - Time allowing: up to 1300 seconds.",
